frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-20-  { text: `There is a room in Minnesota called the anechoic chamber at Orfield Laboratories that is so quiet, the longest anyone has endured it in darkness is forty-five minutes. In total silence, you begin to hear your own heartbeat, then your lungs, then the blood rushing through your veins. Eventually your brain, desperate for input, begins to hallucinate sound. Silence, it turns out, is something the human mind was never designed to experience.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['minimal', 'dark_horror'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-21-  { text: `Octopuses have three hearts, blue blood, and a brain that wraps around their esophagus. Two-thirds of their neurons live in their arms, meaning each arm can taste, touch, and make decisions independently. If you cut off an octopus arm, it will continue to grab food and try to bring it to a mouth that is no longer there. Scientists still argue over whether this counts as consciousness.`, type: 'Nature', styles: ['nature_doc', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-22-  { text: `The human brain consumes twenty watts of power \u2014 less than a dim light bulb \u2014 yet it runs a simulation of reality so detailed you forget it's a simulation. Everything you see, hear, and touch is a reconstruction. Your brain is locked in a dark, silent skull, and it builds the entire world from electrical signals. Color doesn't exist outside your mind. Neither does sound. You are living inside a model, and you have never once stepped outside it.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['documentary', 'sci_fi'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-23-  { text: `There are more possible configurations of a chess game than atoms in the observable universe. The number is called the Shannon number \u2014 roughly ten to the power of one hundred twenty. It means that every game of chess ever played, across all of human history, represents a vanishingly small fraction of what the board can produce. Most possible chess games have never been played and never will be.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['minimal', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-24-  { text: `Neutron stars are so dense that a teaspoon of their material weighs roughly six billion tons \u2014 about the same as every car on Earth compressed into a sugar cube. They spin up to seven hundred times per second, emit beams of radiation from their magnetic poles, and warp spacetime around them so severely that the back of the star is visible from the front. They are the corpses of dead suns, and they are stranger than fiction.`, type: 'Space', styles: ['sci_fi', 'cinematic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:25:  { text: `In nineteen eighty-three, a Soviet officer named Stanislav Petrov received an alert that five American nuclear missiles were heading toward Russia. Protocol demanded he report it immediately, which would have triggered a full retaliatory launch. Instead, he hesitated. He reasoned that a real first strike would involve hundreds of missiles, not five. He reported a system malfunction. He was right. A satellite had misread sunlight reflecting off clouds. One man's hesitation may have prevented the end of civilization.`, type: 'History', styles: ['cinematic', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-26-  { text: `In two thousand twelve, a man named Harrison Okene survived for three days trapped at the bottom of the Atlantic Ocean. His tugboat had capsized in a storm off the coast of Nigeria, sinking to a depth of thirty meters. Harrison found an air pocket in the bathroom \u2014 four feet of breathable space above dark, freezing water. He crouched in pitch blackness for sixty hours, listening to sea creatures around him, until a rescue diver's hand touched his in the dark. The diver screamed. No one expected to find anyone alive.`, type: 'Survival', styles: ['cinematic', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-27-  { text: `A woman named Juliane Koepcke fell two miles out of the sky after her plane was struck by lightning over the Peruvian Amazon in nineteen seventy-one. She was seventeen years old, still strapped to her seat. She survived the fall, woke up alone in the jungle with a broken collarbone and a torn ligament, and walked for eleven days through the rainforest following a stream. She passed crocodiles and insects burrowing into her wounds before finding a logging camp. She was the sole survivor of ninety-two passengers.`, type: 'Survival', styles: ['cinematic', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-28-  { text: `In nineteen sixty-six, a fishing boat off the coast of Iceland sank in freezing water. All crew members died of hypothermia within minutes \u2014 except one. A man named Gulli treaded water at two degrees Celsius for six hours, then walked barefoot across jagged lava rock for three more hours to reach a farm. Scientists later studied his body and discovered his fat composition was structurally similar to seal blubber. His survival rewrote what we knew about human cold tolerance.`, type: 'Survival', styles: ['cinematic', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-29-  { text: `There is a man in India named Dashrath Manjhi who spent twenty-two years carving a road through a mountain \u2014 alone, using only a hammer and chisel. His wife had died because the nearest hospital was on the other side of the mountain, seventy kilometers by road. He started cutting in nineteen sixty and finished in nineteen eighty-two. The path he carved reduced the distance to one kilometer. The government paved it after his death and named it after him.`, type: 'History', styles: ['cinematic', 'motivational'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-30-  { text: `In two thousand ten, thirty-three Chilean miners were trapped seven hundred meters underground after a tunnel collapse. They survived for sixty-nine days in a shelter the size of a small apartment. For the first seventeen days, no one on the surface knew if they were alive. They rationed two spoonfuls of tuna per man per day. When a drill finally broke through, the miners attached a note: "We are fine in the shelter, the thirty-three of us." The rescue operation was watched by over a billion people worldwide.`, type: 'Survival', styles: ['cinematic', 'documentary'] },
--
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-193-  { text: `There is a number called zero that took humanity thousands of years to invent. The Babylonians, Greeks, and Romans had no concept of it. The ancient Greeks debated whether nothing could be something — the philosopher Parmenides argued that the void could not exist. It was Indian mathematicians, around the fifth century, who first treated zero as a number rather than an absence. Brahmagupta wrote the first rules for arithmetic with zero in six twenty-eight AD. The concept traveled to the Arab world, then to Europe, where it was initially banned — the city of Florence outlawed Hindu-Arabic numerals in twelve ninety-nine, fearing that zero made fraud too easy. The most revolutionary number in history is the one that represents nothing.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['minimal', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-194-  { text: `The Monty Hall problem is a probability puzzle that makes mathematicians angry. You are on a game show with three doors. Behind one is a car. Behind the other two are goats. You pick a door. The host, who knows what is behind each door, opens one of the other doors to reveal a goat. He asks: do you want to switch? Most people say it doesn't matter. They are wrong. Switching gives you a two-thirds chance of winning. Staying gives you one-third. When Marilyn vos Savant published the correct answer in Parade magazine in nineteen ninety, she received ten thousand letters of disagreement — many from PhD mathematicians who were certain she was wrong. She was right. The problem reveals that human intuition about probability is fundamentally broken.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['documentary', 'minimal'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-195-  { text: `The number pi has been calculated to over one hundred trillion digits, and no pattern has ever been found. It is almost certainly a normal number, meaning every possible sequence of digits appears somewhere in its decimal expansion — your phone number, your birthday, the complete works of Shakespeare encoded in binary. If this is true, then pi contains every piece of information that has ever existed or ever will exist, buried somewhere in an infinite, patternless string. It is the mathematical equivalent of the Library of Babel — everything is in there, and none of it is findable. Pi is not a number. It is a universe disguised as a ratio.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['documentary', 'sci_fi'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-196-  { text: `There is a mathematical proof that there are more real numbers between zero and one than there are whole numbers in all of infinity. Georg Cantor proved this in eighteen seventy-four and it nearly cost him his sanity. His colleagues called his work a disease. Leopold Kronecker called him a “corrupter of youth.” Cantor spent the last years of his life in and out of psychiatric hospitals. But he was right. Not all infinities are the same size. The infinity of counting numbers is smaller than the infinity of real numbers. There are levels of infinity, stacked on top of each other, going up forever. Cantor opened a door that mathematics has never been able to close.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['minimal', 'documentary'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-197-  { text: `The Sagrada Família in Barcelona has been under construction for over one hundred forty years. Antoni Gaudí began the project in eighteen eighty-two, knowing he would never see it finished. When asked about the slow pace, he said, “My client is not in a hurry” — meaning God. Gaudí spent the last twelve years of his life living on the construction site, sleeping in his workshop, growing increasingly gaunt and obsessive. He was struck by a tram in nineteen twenty-six and died three days later. He was so poorly dressed that passersby assumed he was a beggar. The church is scheduled for completion in twenty twenty-six — the centennial of his death. It will have taken longer to build than the Great Pyramid.`, type: 'History', styles: ['cinematic', 'gothic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js:198:  { text: `The Panama Canal required the removal of more earth than any construction project in history — over two hundred million cubic meters of rock and soil. The French tried first, starting in eighteen eighty-one. They failed catastrophically. Over twenty-two thousand workers died, mostly from malaria and yellow fever. The project bankrupted the French company and triggered a political scandal that nearly toppled the government. The Americans succeeded two decades later, largely because Colonel William Gorgas figured out that the diseases were carried by mosquitoes and drained the swamps. The canal is not just an engineering triumph. It is a monument to the twenty-two thousand people whose deaths taught the survivors that the real enemy was never the mountain — it was the puddles.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-199-  { text: `The Hoover Dam contains enough concrete to build a two-lane highway from San Francisco to New York. It was poured in interlocking columns rather than a single mass because a solid block of that size would have taken one hundred twenty-five years to cool and would have cracked from internal heat. The concrete at its core is still curing to this day. Twenty-one thousand men worked on it during the Great Depression, and ninety-six died during construction — though the official number was kept lower. The dam's generators provide electricity to one point three million people. It holds back nine point two trillion gallons of water. If it ever failed, the flood would reach Mexico.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-200-  { text: `During World War Two, the British created an entire fictional army — the First United States Army Group — to convince Hitler that the D-Day invasion would land at Calais instead of Normandy. They assigned General George Patton, Germany's most feared Allied commander, to lead it. They built inflatable tanks, plywood aircraft, and fake radio traffic. Double agents fed Germany confirming intelligence. The deception was so successful that even after the Normandy landings began, Hitler held his reserve Panzer divisions at Calais for seven weeks, waiting for the “real” invasion. The most important army in the war never existed.`, type: 'History', styles: ['war_military', 'vintage_retro'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-201-  { text: `The CIA once spent twenty million dollars building a mechanical dragonfly to carry a listening device. Project Insectothopter, developed in the nineteen seventies, was a tiny unmanned aerial vehicle designed to fly into embassies and land near conversations. It worked in the lab. In the field, it couldn't handle even a light crosswind. The project was shelved. Decades later, the dragonfly was declassified and put on display at the CIA Museum, which itself is classified — located inside CIA headquarters in Langley, Virginia, and closed to the public. The most advanced spy technology of the Cold War is now a museum piece inside a museum that no one is allowed to visit.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'noir'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-202-  { text: `During the Cold War, the Soviets built an entire fake city in the Ural Mountains to deceive American spy satellites. Dummy buildings, roads, and infrastructure were constructed to look like a military installation from above. The Americans, in turn, built a fake suburban neighborhood in the Nevada desert — complete with houses, cars, and mannequin families — to test the effects of nuclear weapons on civilian areas. Both superpowers spent billions constructing elaborate fictions for each other's cameras. The Cold War was, in many ways, the most expensive theater production in human history — two nations performing for an audience of spy satellites.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'noir'] },
frontend/src\shared\data\stories.js-203-  { text: `The deepest point in the ocean is the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench — nearly eleven thousand meters below the surface. The pressure there is over a thousand times atmospheric pressure. If you dropped a steel ball from the surface, it would take over an hour to reach the bottom. Only three expeditions have carried humans to the floor. The first, in nineteen sixty, was Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh in the bathyscaphe Trieste. They spent twenty minutes on the bottom and saw, to everyone's astonishment, a flatfish — proof that life exists in the most extreme environment on the planet. More people have walked on the Moon than have visited the deepest point of the ocean beneath their feet.`, type: 'Nature', styles: ['nature_doc', 'cinematic'] },
