frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-109-  { text: `There is a psychological phenomenon called the Baader-Meinhof effect where, after you learn a new word or concept, you suddenly start seeing it everywhere. You buy a red car and suddenly every third car on the road is red. You learn the word "sonder" and find it in three articles that week. Your brain hasn't changed the world. It has changed what it pays attention to. Your reticular activating system — the brain's filter for relevance — just added a new keyword to its search. Reality didn't shift. Your perception did. And you will never be able to unsee what you've now been trained to notice.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['surreal', 'documentary'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-110-  { text: `There is a region of the Pacific Ocean called the Oceanic Pole of Inaccessibility — also known as Point Nemo. It is the spot on Earth farthest from any landmass, surrounded by over a thousand miles of open water in every direction. The nearest humans are often the astronauts aboard the International Space Station, orbiting two hundred fifty miles overhead. Space agencies use it as a spacecraft graveyard — over two hundred sixty deorbited satellites and space stations rest at the bottom. It is the loneliest point on the planet, and the sky above it is closer to civilization than the sea in any direction.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['cinematic', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-111-  { text: `The peak-end rule explains why you remember experiences wrong. Your brain doesn't average an experience — it judges it almost entirely by its most intense moment and how it ended. A colonoscopy that lasted longer but ended gently was rated as less painful than a shorter one that ended abruptly. A vacation with one magical evening and a smooth departure will be remembered more fondly than two weeks of moderate fun with a stressful airport experience. You can engineer someone's memory of an event by controlling the peak and the ending. Everything in between barely matters.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-112-  { text: `The pratfall effect says that competent people become more likable when they make a small mistake. A candidate who spills coffee during a flawless presentation is rated as more approachable and trustworthy than one who is impeccable throughout. But it only works if you're already seen as capable — a clumsy mistake from an incompetent person makes them less likable. The lesson is counterintuitive: perfection creates distance. A well-timed vulnerability makes competence feel human. The most likable version of you is not the perfect version. It's the excellent version that occasionally drops things.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-113-  { text: `The mere exposure effect means you will grow to like almost anything you see often enough. Faces, songs, logos, foods — repeated exposure breeds familiarity, and familiarity breeds preference. This is why radio stations play the same songs until you like them. Why you gravitate toward people you see regularly. Why brands plaster their logos on every surface. The first time you hear a new song, you might hate it. By the tenth time, you're humming it. By the fiftieth, it's your favorite. Preference is not a choice. It's a frequency.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-114-  { text: `The contrast principle is why salespeople always show you the most expensive option first. After seeing a ten-thousand-dollar watch, a two-thousand-dollar watch feels like a bargain. After lifting a heavy suitcase, a light bag feels weightless. Your brain doesn't evaluate things in absolute terms — it evaluates everything relative to what came immediately before. Real estate agents show the worst house first so the second house looks like a palace. The contrast isn't between options. It's between perceptions. And the person who controls the sequence controls the decision.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'dark_psychology'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-115-  { text: `The sunk cost fallacy is why you finish bad movies, stay in failing relationships, and eat food you don't want just because you paid for it. Your brain treats past investment — time, money, emotion — as a reason to keep going, even when quitting is the rational choice. A company will pour millions into a doomed project because admitting failure means admitting the millions were wasted. The fallacy isn't logical — it's emotional. Walking away feels like losing twice. But the money is gone either way. The only question that matters is: would you start this from zero today? If the answer is no, the past is irrelevant.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'motivational'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-116-  { text: `Cognitive dissonance is the discomfort you feel when your actions contradict your beliefs — and your brain's desperate attempt to resolve it by changing the belief rather than the behavior. Smokers who can't quit convince themselves the risks are exaggerated. People who hurt someone convince themselves the person deserved it. After paying a hundred dollars for a mediocre meal, you tell yourself it was actually quite good. Your brain would rather rewrite your values than admit you made a mistake. You are not as rational as you think. You are a story your brain tells itself to feel consistent.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'dark_psychology'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-117-  { text: `The bystander effect is not about apathy — it's about diffusion of responsibility. When you witness an emergency alone, you act. But when fifty people witness the same emergency, each person assumes someone else will help. The result is that nobody helps. In the famous case of Kitty Genovese, thirty-eight witnesses heard her being attacked and not one called police — not because they didn't care, but because each assumed another neighbor already had. The fix is brutally simple: point at one person and say "You, call nine one one." Specificity shatters the diffusion. Name someone, and they become responsible.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-118-  { text: `The Dunning-Kruger effect is not just about stupid people thinking they're smart. It's about a blind spot that affects everyone. When you know very little about a subject, you lack the knowledge to recognize your own ignorance — so you overestimate yourself. But as you learn more, you realize how much you don't know, and your confidence drops. Experts underestimate themselves because they assume everyone knows what they know. The most dangerous person in any room is not the one who knows nothing — it's the one who knows just enough to feel certain. True expertise feels like doubt. Ignorance feels like confidence.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-119-  { text: `Social proof is the reason you check reviews before buying anything, why laugh tracks make sitcoms funnier, and why an empty restaurant feels suspicious even if the food is excellent. Your brain uses other people's behavior as a shortcut for decision-making. If everyone else is doing it, it must be correct. Bartenders seed their tip jars. Charities publish donor lists. Testimonials sell more than features. In one experiment, a sign in a hotel bathroom saying "most guests reuse their towels" increased towel reuse by twenty-six percent — more than any environmental message. You don't follow logic. You follow the crowd. And you rarely notice you're doing it.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'dark_psychology'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-120-  { text: `The paradox of choice explains why more options make you less happy. When you have two jams to choose from, you pick one and enjoy it. When you have thirty, you agonize over the decision, second-guess your choice, and enjoy the result less — because the existence of twenty-nine alternatives makes you wonder if you chose wrong. Barry Schwartz found that people with more options report lower satisfaction across careers, relationships, and purchases. Freedom feels like a gift until it becomes a burden. The happiest people are not those with the most choices. They're the ones who have learned to stop looking after they choose.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:121:  { text: `There is a tree in Pando, Utah, that is actually forty-seven thousand individual trunks sharing a single root system. It is one organism. It weighs roughly thirteen million pounds, making it the heaviest known living thing on Earth. It has been alive for an estimated eighty thousand years — older than every human civilization, every written language, every religion. When the first humans walked out of Africa, Pando was already ancient. It has survived ice ages, droughts, and fires. It is dying now, slowly, because deer eat its young shoots and humans suppress the wildfires it needs to regenerate.`, type: 'Nature', styles: ['nature_doc', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-122-  { text: `There is a deep-sea fish called the barreleye that has a completely transparent head. You can see its brain through its skull. Its tubular green eyes sit inside its fluid-filled dome and can rotate upward to scan for the silhouettes of prey above. It lives in the twilight zone of the ocean, between six hundred and eight hundred meters deep, where almost no sunlight reaches. It was first described in nineteen thirty-nine, but no one saw a living specimen with its transparent shield intact until two thousand four, because the dome collapsed every time one was brought to the surface.`, type: 'Nature', styles: ['nature_doc', 'surreal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-123-  { text: `There is a lake beneath the Antarctic ice sheet called Lake Vostok that has been sealed off from the outside world for roughly fifteen million years. It sits beneath two and a half miles of ice. When scientists drilled down and sampled the water in twenty twelve, they found microbial life — organisms that had evolved in total isolation, in perpetual darkness, under crushing pressure, for longer than humans have existed as a species. Whatever lives in Lake Vostok has never seen sunlight, never breathed atmospheric air, and has no idea that an entire world exists above the ice.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['sci_fi', 'nature_doc'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-124-  { text: `The pistol shrimp snaps its claw so fast it creates a bubble that reaches four thousand seven hundred degrees Celsius — nearly as hot as the surface of the sun. The collapsing bubble also produces a flash of light called sonoluminescence. For a fraction of a nanosecond, the shrimp generates a tiny star in the ocean. The snap is two hundred eighteen decibels, louder than a gunshot. Colonies of pistol shrimp produce so much noise that they interfere with naval sonar. The ocean floor is patrolled by one-inch creatures wielding miniature suns.`, type: 'Nature', styles: ['nature_doc', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-125-  { text: `The gray rock method is a defense strategy against narcissists and manipulators. You become as boring and unresponsive as a gray rock — short answers, no emotional reactions, no interesting stories. Narcissists feed on drama and emotional responses. When you become bland, you starve them. They lose interest and move on to more reactive targets. The technique works because manipulators don't actually want you — they want your emotional energy. Remove the supply, and the predator finds easier prey. It is the psychological equivalent of playing dead.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-126-  { text: `There is a manipulation pattern called DARVO — Deny, Attack, Reverse Victim and Offender. When confronted with their behavior, the manipulator first denies everything. Then they attack the person confronting them. Finally, they reverse the roles — claiming they are the real victim and you are the aggressor. "I never said that. Why are you always attacking me? You're the one being abusive." It is devastatingly effective because it forces the accuser to defend themselves instead of pressing the original issue. The conversation shifts from their behavior to your character. By the time you realize what happened, the original issue has evaporated.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'noir'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-127-  { text: `The concept of "flying monkeys" comes from The Wizard of Oz, but psychologists use it to describe people a narcissist recruits to do their bidding. The narcissist never confronts you directly — instead, they tell their version of events to friends, family, and coworkers, who then pressure you on the narcissist's behalf. The flying monkeys believe they're helping. They don't realize they're being weaponized. The narcissist maintains clean hands while others deliver the punishment. Isolation is the goal — one by one, your support network is turned into surveillance.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'dark_horror'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-128-  { text: `Emotional vampires are not a metaphor in psychology — they're a clinical observation. Certain personality types systematically drain the emotional energy of everyone around them. They dominate conversations, manufacture crises, and create a gravitational pull of need that leaves you exhausted after every interaction. The five types identified by psychiatrist Albert Bernstein are the narcissist, the antisocial, the histrionic, the obsessive-compulsive, and the paranoid. The common thread is that they take more emotional energy than they give, and they do it so consistently that the people around them burn out without understanding why.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'gothic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-129-  { text: `The city of Constantinople was conquered in fourteen fifty-three partly because of a single unlocked gate. The Ottoman army had besieged the city for fifty-three days with the largest cannon ever built, but the walls held. Then someone discovered that a small postern gate called the Kerkoporta had been left open — possibly by accident, possibly by treachery. A handful of Ottoman soldiers slipped through, raised their flag on the inner wall, and the defenders panicked. The greatest fortified city in history, which had withstood twenty-three sieges over a thousand years, fell because of one door.`, type: 'History', styles: ['war_military', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-130-  { text: `There is a radio signal from space called the fast radio burst that releases more energy in a millisecond than the sun produces in an entire day. The first one was detected in two thousand seven in archived data from two thousand one. They come from billions of light-years away, last only a few milliseconds, and most never repeat. In twenty twenty, scientists traced one to a magnetar — a neutron star with a magnetic field a quadrillion times stronger than Earth's. But many bursts remain unexplained. Something out there is screaming across the universe in millisecond bursts of impossible energy, and we are only beginning to hear it.`, type: 'Space', styles: ['sci_fi', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-131-  { text: `There are more bacteria in your mouth right now than there are people on Earth. Roughly seven hundred species of microbes colonize the human oral cavity, and at any given moment, between one and ten billion individual bacteria inhabit your mouth. After you brush your teeth, the population drops to about one hundred million — and recovers within hours. Your mouth is one of the most biodiverse ecosystems on the planet. Some of these bacteria protect you. Others, left unchecked, will dissolve your teeth from the inside out. You are hosting a civilization and a war simultaneously.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['documentary', 'nature_doc'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-132-  { text: `There is a place in Turkmenistan called the Door to Hell — a crater seventy meters wide that has been burning continuously since nineteen seventy-one. Soviet geologists were drilling for natural gas when the ground collapsed, creating a massive sinkhole filled with methane. They set it on fire, expecting it to burn out in a few weeks. It has been burning for over fifty years. The crater glows orange at night and can be seen from miles away in the desert. In twenty twenty-two, the Turkmen president ordered it extinguished for environmental reasons. It is still burning.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['cinematic', 'dark_horror'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-133-  { text: `In twenty nineteen, scientists captured the first photograph of a black hole — a supermassive object at the center of galaxy Messier eighty-seven, fifty-five million light-years from Earth. The image required a telescope the size of the planet — eight radio observatories on four continents synchronized to the precision of a billionth of a second, collecting five petabytes of data that took two years to process. The orange ring in the image is not the black hole itself — it is light bent around the event horizon, the boundary beyond which nothing escapes. You are looking at the edge of the unknowable.`, type: 'Space', styles: ['sci_fi', 'cinematic'] },
